---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import LifeTimeline from '../components/LifeTimeline.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '');

const facts = [
  ['Base', 'Lake Stevens'],
  ['Training', 'CPA / GEOINT'],
  ['Thread', 'Systems'],
];

const chapters = [
  ['00', 'Timeline', '#timeline'],
  ['01', 'Systems', '#why-this-combination-matters'],
  ['02', 'Map', '#the-map-on-the-porch'],
  ['03', 'Comeback', '#dropout-comeback'],
  ['04', 'Off the clock', '#off-the-clock'],
  ['05', 'Building', '#what-ive-been-building'],
];
---

<Base
  current="about"
  title="Story | Alex Schulz"
  description="An unconventional path through live production, family offices, real estate, aerospace, geospatial data, and software—and what it taught Alex Schulz about systems."
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="shell pt-12 md:pt-20">
    <p class="eyebrow">Story</p>
    <h1 class="display mt-4 max-w-3xl text-5xl md:text-7xl">The long version.</h1>
    <p class="lede mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg md:text-xl">
      An unconventional path through live production, family offices, real estate, aerospace,
      geospatial data, and software—and what it taught me about systems.
    </p>

    <dl class="mt-10 grid grid-cols-3 divide-x divide-rule-soft border-y border-rule">
      {facts.map(([label, value]) => (
        <div class="py-4 pl-3 pr-2 first:pl-0 md:px-5 md:first:pl-0">
          <dt class="eyebrow">{label}</dt>
          <dd class="display mt-1 text-lg sm:text-2xl md:text-3xl">{value}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <div class="mt-8 grid grid-cols-1 gap-5 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_22rem]">
      <div class="plate p-6 md:p-8">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-signal">The short version</p>
        <p class="lede mt-3 text-base md:text-lg">
          I'm a CPA with ten years of accounting experience across live production, family
          offices, real-estate development, aerospace, and marketplace operations. During the
          pandemic I added a graduate certificate in digital mapping and a master's in geospatial
          intelligence — both 4.0, both nights and weekends. Now I build working apps, data pipelines,
          and accounting automation with AI, and show where the numbers come from.
          The long version below is a better story.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-6 border-t border-rule-soft pt-5">
          <p class="eyebrow">Working habit</p>
          <p class="copy mt-2 text-base">
            I get pulled toward the useful detail sitting just outside the frame: the map margin,
            the rail spur, the filing footnote, the small operational clue that changes the larger
            story.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="plate self-start p-3">
        <div class="px-2 pb-3 pt-1">
          <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-signal">Soundtrack</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Jadakiss - Things I've Been Through</p>
        </div>
        <iframe
          class="block h-[152px] w-full border-0"
          title="Spotify Embed: Jadakiss - Things I've Been Through"
          src="https://open.spotify.com/embed/track/1nzOHmHda8NbuwGJjFz5fP?utm_source=generator&theme=0"
          allow="autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; fullscreen; picture-in-picture"
          loading="lazy"
        ></iframe>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <LifeTimeline />

  <!-- The essay, with a contents rail. The timeline above tells each stop once; this covers the rest. -->
  <section class="shell py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="atlas-rule"><span>The long version</span></div>

    <div class="mt-10 grid grid-cols-1 gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_15rem] lg:gap-16">
      <nav aria-label="Story chapters" class="lg:order-2 lg:sticky lg:top-24 lg:self-start">
        <p class="eyebrow">Contents</p>
        <ol class="mt-3 grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-6 border-t border-rule-soft sm:grid-cols-3 lg:grid-cols-1">
          {chapters.map(([number, label, href]) => (
            <li class="border-b border-rule-soft">
              <a href={href} class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2 text-sm text-ink-soft transition-colors hover:text-signal">
                <span class="plate-roman text-xs">{number}</span>
                <span>{label}</span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <div class="prose-atlas max-w-2xl text-[1.0625rem] lg:order-1">
        <h2 id="why-this-combination-matters" class="!mt-0">Why the range matters.</h2>
        <p>
          These chapters exposed me to organizations with very different customers, assets,
          constraints, and definitions of success. A film production, a family office, a real-estate
          developer, a launch-services company, and a software marketplace do not look alike from the
          outside.
        </p>
        <p>
          Inside, many of the same system problems repeat: fragmented data, manual handoffs,
          undocumented rules, approval bottlenecks, exceptions that consume disproportionate time,
          and important knowledge that lives in people rather than software.
        </p>
        <p>
          Accounting taught me to reconcile and preserve evidence. Geospatial work taught me to
          acquire, transform, and question difficult data. Product work taught me to translate an
          idea into requirements, tradeoffs, tested behavior, and a usable interface. AI now compresses
          the distance between those forms of judgment and a working system.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-map-on-the-porch">The map on the porch.</h2>
        <Figure
          figure={{
            src: 'images/industrial-map-of-washington.jpeg',
            alt: 'Framed Industrial Map of the State of Washington, circa 1940, from the family dairy farm in Custer, WA',
            caption: "Industrial Map of Washington, from my mom's grandparents' dairy farm in Custer, Washington.",
            ratio: '4/3',
          }}
          label="Frontispiece"
        />
        <p>
          One of the objects that made maps feel personal was an old Industrial Map of Washington
          from my mom's grandparents' dairy farm in Custer, Washington. It had been hanging on the
          porch by a thumb tack until my mom took it home and framed it.
        </p>
        <p>
          I think that is part of why my work keeps circling back to maps: they show industry,
          movement, land, and memory at once. They are how hidden systems become visible.
        </p>
        <p>
          Land records became personal in another way, too. I twice helped my mom defend the
          boundaries of her own property — an unwanted education in documentation, recorded facts,
          and the gap between being right and being able to prove it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="dropout-comeback">Dropout, comeback.</h2>
        <p>
          The dropout and the comeback happened in the same five years.
          I've earned a 4.0 in every program I've completed since.
        </p>
        <p>
          My twenties had a strange momentum to them. I kept finding myself in rooms I would not have
          known how to ask for: backstage in Hawaii, a Wall Street forum, billionaire family offices,
          film and television production. My thirties were different. The doors did not open as
          easily and life got heavier. That chapter gave me a different kind of education: endurance,
          documentation, and the need to build a direction instead of waiting for one.
        </p>

        <h2 id="off-the-clock">Off the clock.</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Tennis.</strong> USTA 4.0. In 2024 I reached the final of all seven 4.0 doubles
          tournaments I entered, won four including the Pacific Northwest sectionals, and was ranked
          third in the nation that December. When my partner was bumped up to 4.5, I played up too, at
          4.5 nationals in Orlando. Tennis is also why I built
          a shot-by-shot study of 1.85 million professional
          points, and a model that finds tennis courts in
          federal aerial imagery.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Golf.</strong> One hole-in-one: the 11th at Bear Mountain Ranch in Chelan, 175 yards,
          with a 7-iron, in August 2023. Golf also produced my 2022 map of green fees across
          Western Washington, one of the early maps that show
          where the mapping work started.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-ive-been-building">What I've been building.</h2>
        <p>
          <strong><a href={`${base}/work/national-parcel-coverage`}>National Parcel Coverage</a></strong>
          is a national parcel store assembled from free public sources: 143.9 million records across
          2,613 counties, in one schema.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong><a href={`${base}/work/close-automation`}>Controlled Close Automation</a></strong>
          is an internal finance-operations cockpit, built in my role at Taskrabbit, that turns
          recurring close tasks into validated, reviewable, and auditable workflows.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong><a href={`${base}/work/private-trip-sharing`}>Private Trip Sharing</a></strong>
          is a private trip app built for one family vacation, used again for a second, and now being
          rebuilt as a product.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong><a href={`${base}/work/item2`}>Item2</a></strong> turns SEC facility disclosures
          into a versioned physical-asset graph with structured AI extraction, geocoding, authority
          matching, provenance, and human review.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong><a href={`${base}/work/wa-rail-properties`}>WA Rail-Served Properties</a></strong>
          began as the Maryland capstone: an inventory of every parcel in Washington State with
          private rail access.
        </p>
        <p>
          How I run finance work with an AI agent has <a href={`${base}/method`}>its own page</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          I am now bringing these threads together in what I build with AI: working apps, data
          pipelines, and accounting automation. If any of this lands for you, <a href="mailto:alex.j.schulz@gmail.com">write</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTA />
</Base>
